     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of f justice and ensure greater accessibility to justice related services and activities</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The automation of the court procedures through the promotion of e-justice has enhanced the efficiency of the court and prosecutor office due to the reduction of paper work and increased use of computers. UNDP support to MoJ has totally transformed court procedures and operations from time-taking paper-based, manual operation to a computer-based system. By centralising all legal service tasks within one office, the new system has already reduced the burden of the public. It also resulted in significant reduction of the time required to deliver the services such as issuing power of attorney and court rulings, and authentication of documents (E.g. Power of attorney can now be obtained in less 20 minutes, which represents significant reduction in time and cost for service clients). This in turn resulted in greater satisfaction of service users, enhanced staff morale and improved working environment. The fully automated case flow management system is expected to result in improved operational efficiency, better reporting, and reduced backlo</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of f justice and ensure greater accessibility to justice related services and activities</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP helped the Ministry of Justice (MOJ) to strengthen its human and institutional capacity to improve access to justice and promote and protect human rights. The support is well aligned with the strategic plan of the Ministry which prioritizes human resource development through distance education and on-the-job training, improving professional and research capacity of the sector, and enhancing participation of the society in improving
access to justice. Supported the enrollment of 93 (30% women) staff in LLM course with the UNISA drawn from the Judiciary, Office of the Prosecutor, Legal Services, etc.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of f justice and ensure greater accessibility to justice related services and activities</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP helped the Ministry of Justice (MOJ) to strengthen its human and institutional capacity to improve access to justice and promote and protect human rights. The support is well aligned with the strategic plan of the Ministry which prioritizes human resource development through distance education and on-the-job training, improving professional and research capacity of the sector, and enhancing participation of the society in improving
access to justice. Supported the enrollment of 93 (30% women) staff in LLM course with the UNISA drawn from the Judiciary, Office of the Prosecutor, Legal Services, etc.

UNDP in association with the Ministry of Justice in Eritrea, supported by the British Embassy, convened a five day dialogue on International Law. The dialogue took place in Asmara, Eritrea at the National Confederation of Eritrean Workers (NCEW) Conference Centre from the 6th to the 10th of June, 2022. 
The main objective of the dialogue was to unpack several concepts on international law with a view to understand possible convergence and points of departure in respect of the Eritrean context, believes and value systems. Any alignment with international law standards has to inculcate the Eritrean context and value-based approach to the human right to dignity and self-worth. This dialogue was seen as a process to pave the path towards this potential alignment. Sub-objectives of the dialogue was to empower officials on the various international law standards and set a tone for more internal dialogues and banter on various justice related legal concepts and its applicability within the ministry and its impact on justice related services.  
50 officials from the Ministry of Justice from across the country and representing a cross section of the Ministry services ranging from Judges of the Appeal Court, Judges of the local division, Prosecution, Office of the Attorney General, Court Services and the Minister&#x2019;s Advisory team participated. This team is considered to be the brain trust of the Administration of Justice Services and serve in various parts of the justice system and well positioned to engage on national policy issues.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
